my name is hannah and this is about the  dependent samples t-test by watching  this tutorial you will easily understand  what a dependent samples t-test is  how you can calculate and interpret it  and what the requirements are and at the  end of this video i will show you how  you can easily calculate this t-test  online with data type if you want to  look up the topics of this video just  take a look at datadept.net  there you will find all the informations  so let's get started  the t-test for dependent samples tests  whether the mean value of two dependent  samples  significantly differ from each other  what is a dependent sample  in the case of dependent samples  the measured values are away level in  pairs the pairs result for example by  measurement repetitions with the same  persons  let's look at an example  let's say you want to find out if  vacations have an influence on the  stress level of people  in order to analyze this question you  have created a small online  questionnaire with datatab.net  by using this questionnaire  you ask people before and after their  vacation about their stress level  there are now two ways to do this  possibility one is you interview a group  of people before they go on vacation  and then you ask an other group of  people  after they come back from a vacation  in this case you would have an  independent sample because the people  you interviewed before the vacation  have nothing to do with the people you  interviewed after the vacation  the second possibility and also the  better possibility is you ask people  before they go on vacation and then you  ask the exactly same group of people  again  when they return from their vacation  in this case you would have a dependent  sample so the measured values are always  available in pairs so you have two  values for exactly the same person  so therefore if the people have nothing  in common with each other you have an  independent sample  if the people you ask before and after  are exactly the same people  the measurements are in pairs and you  have a dependent sample  in this case the t-test for dependent  samples is used  before you can calculate a t-test of  course you must formulate your  hypotheses  in the case of the dependent t test the  null hypothesis is  there is no significant difference  between the means of the two dependent  groups  and your alternative hypothesis would be  there is a significant difference  between the means of the two dependent  groups  therefore  one hypothesis is that there is no  difference and the other hypothesis says  that there is a difference  of course it's also possible to  formulate directed or one-sided  hypotheses  then the null hypothesis would be  one sample has a significantly smaller  or equal mean value  and the alternative hypothesis would be  one sample has a significantly larger  mean value  of course the smaller and larger can  also be reversed depending on your  research question  how does the dependency test work  of course we'll look at this using an  example to recap the dependent samples  t-test tests whether the means of two  dependent samples are significantly  different from each other  let's say you want to test whether a  diet program developed by a celebrity  really has an effect on people's weight  to do this you find individuals who are  willing to follow the diet program  you measure the weight of the test  persons once before and once after the  diet program  since you measure the weight of the test  persons once before and once after the  diet program the measured values are  always in pairs and that's why you have  a dependent sample  your null hypothesis is the diet program  has no significant effect on the body  weight of the participants  and your alternative hypothesis is the  diet program has a significant effect  on the body weight of participants  of course you could also make directed  hypotheses  your alternative hypothesis then would  either be  the diet program significantly reduces  the weight  or the diet program significantly  increases the weight of the individuals  but enough about these hypotheses  how do we calculate the t-test for  independent samples  first you start by weighing each test  person once before and once after the  completed diet program  we are not interested in the weight  before and after but we are interested  in the difference between before and  after  so for example how much a person has  gained or lost weight  therefore we calculate the difference  between before and after for each person  by doing this we have converted the two  paired values into  one new value  by this conversion we have made one data  set out of the two data sets  now that we only have one data set  the t-test for dependent sample is  equivalent to the t-test for one sample  let's say the first person gained three  kilos the second lost four kilos the  third one lost seven kilos and the  fourth person  gained five kilos  so on average the participants lost  0.75 kilos  now the big question is whether the diet  is responsible for the fact that there  is a difference or whether this  difference occurred by chance  and the diet has actually no influence  to answer this question you now need the  t-test for dependent samples  now some of you will certainly shout  stop what about the requirements don't  we have to check them first that is  absolutely correct however first i would  like to explain the basics to you by  using an example and then we will go  into the requirements  to calculate the t-test there are now  two possibilities either you calculate  the t value by hand and compare it with  the so-called critical t value  we will look at this in a moment  or the second way is you simply use a  statistic software as data tab and let  it calculate and interpret everything  automatically i will show you how to do  this online at the end of this video so  let's start with the calculation by hand  we want to calculate the t test but how  do we get to the t value  in order to calculate the t value  we first need the number of cases  we have four dependent pairs so we have  four cases  the number of degrees of freedom is the  number of cases minus one which makes  three  we calculate the mean of the data simply  by adding up all the values and dividing  them by the number of cases this leads  us to the mean value of minus  0.75  kilos  next thing we need is the standard  deviation  for this data we get a standard  deviation of 5.84  by using the standard deviation and the  number of cases we can now calculate the  standard error of mean which is  2.84  the standard error of mean tells us how  much the mean would vary  if we had taken quite a lot of samples  this means that the larger this value is  the more likely a large weight gain or  loss will occur by chance because there  is simply a large variation in the mean  so and now we can finally calculate the  t value the t value is the mean  minus 0 divided by the standard arrow of  the mean  y zero  our null hypothesis says that there is  no difference  so we want to test if the mean is  significantly different from zero  now we need to check whether the amount  of this calculated t value is smaller or  larger than the so called critical t  value we can find the critical t value  in a table but how does this work  we have set a significance level of five  percent  if we are testing an undirected  hypothesis this would mean that we split  the five percent between the left and  the right side  so we have 2.5 on the left  and 2.5  on the right side  therefore in the table of critical t  values we need to look at  97.5 percent this is what we get by  subtracting half the significance level  from one  so we are in this column right now  the degrees of freedom in our case are  five minus one which makes three so we  are in the table in this row so the  critical t value is 3.182  we've calculated the t value of minus  0.26 therefore the critical 